WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
We asked you guys on Instagram:

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.600
What are the things you have always been wondering about in table tennis?

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.180
In this video we will try to answer the
most asked questions.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:15.040
Why do table tennis players always brush the area
near the net with their hands?

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.000
This is one of the most asked questions on our channel too

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.740
And the answer has two parts

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.220
When you play table tennis

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.440
It's very common that your hand sweats

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.820
because of all the time being in contact with the racket

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.320
So between the points

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.400
It's very usual that players go wipe the sweat off of the hand

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.120
and then continue playing

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.880
And the reason why the hand is often dried here

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.500
is that you don't want the playing area to be wet

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.280
The second part to this answer is that

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.920
drying the hand on the table
is also a mental thing for the player

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.220
So often before the serve or the receive

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.440
Players want to take a moment to refocus

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.440
before the next point

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.240
Why do table tennis rackets have a red and a black side?

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.340
This rule came in the 1980's

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.900
because people were using two different rubbers
but with the same colour

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.480
So when you twiddled the racket
opponent didn't know which one you were using

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.820
For example if you have a pimple on the other side
and a normal rubber on the other side

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.900
It's easier for the opponent to tell which you a using
if they are different colours

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.120
I don't know if there's any particular reason
why it's red and black

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.460
but I think it's mainly because
it's easy to see the difference

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.820
Why are there nearly no pimples and antis
in the higher classes?

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.720
This is a really hard questions

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.200
And that's why we asked the world's best material player Mattias Falck

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.980
what he thinks the reasons are

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.400
Hi, Mattias Falck here!

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.300
That's a hard questions to answer

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.840
I think in the men's side

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.220
I'm the only one who have short pimples of the top 200 in the world

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:53.440
In my opinion it would be good if there were more styles in the top levels too

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.280
To get more changes in the big matches
and the syles of the players

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.660
I think the ball is quite good for short pimples

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.280
It's hard and less spin than before
so it bounces quite high

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.140
For long pimples I don't know

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.380
Hopefully Pongfinity have some better answers than me

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.460
Take care!

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.100
So this is a question I have also wondered myself

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.840
It's very common for everybody
to start with a normal racket

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.280
and then you get better and better with the normal racket

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.240
And then when you have already achieved a certain level

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.500
You don't want to start from the beginning again

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.040
Because starting to play with something
else than an inverted normal rubber

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.900
Usually takes a lot of time, a lot of practice

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.440
And most people don't wanna take the extra time

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.080
so they just stick to the normal one

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.280
Why don't you just play short
when the opponent is far away?

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.500
So it might seem that
when the opponent is far away from the table

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.480
it would be easy to win the point
just by dropping it short

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.480
But making a good drop shot is a lot harder than it looks

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.480
If it's not short and low enough

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.040
it's easy for the opponent to
just come close to the table and win the point

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.140
But if you know how to do it well
and know when to use it

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.720
it can be a really good shot!

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.260
Can you move the table by accident during a point?

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.980
So for obvious reasons it's not allowed
to move the table during a rally

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.160
But it's not even allowed by accident

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.660
The official rules say that you lose a point

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.560
if you, or anything you wear or carry,
moves the playing surface

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.820
Is being left handed an advantage?

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.280
It's not a real advantage

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.160
but usually it's easier
to play against right handed players

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.060
This is simply because there are more right handed players

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.160
so you are more used to
practising and playing against them

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.060
Also when you play against left-handers

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.040
the sidespin comes in the opposite way
and you have to readjust the ball placement

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.760
So there is a small advantage

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.140
So if you can choose
I would recommend playing left handed

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.880
So for obvious reasons it's not allowed to move the table...

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:57.780
Too good!

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.420
Why are you Emil laughing all the time?

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.800
I know I'm quite funny but try to keep it together!

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.900
It was fun and interesting to go through
and answer your questions

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.729
So if you have more of them
just leave them in the comments below

00:04:10.729 --> 00:04:12.800
and we might make another video like this

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:14.500
Until next time!

